course: history
lesson: 87
title: Olympiad: spot the anachronism ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: An anachronism is something put into the wrong time: a knight with a phone, a pharaoh with a wristwatch, a Roman shouting «OK». Films, cartoons and games are full of them, and spotting them is a favourite olympiad puzzle.
explain: To catch one, ask two questions: when does the scene happen, and when did this thing first appear? If the thing is younger than the scene, you have found an anachronism.
explain: The trap is that some things are older than you think. Columbus really did carry a compass, Queen Victoria really did ride on trains, and Abraham Lincoln and Taras Shevchenko really did sit for photographs.
explain: Food is a great clue: potatoes, tomatoes, maize and chocolate came from the Americas, so a Roman or a Viking eating them is wrong, and tea only reached Europe in the 1600s.
explain: Sophie in Bristol watches a Roman film: «Gurt lush, but why's the centurion wearing a wristwatch?» (in the West Country gurt means very and lush means lovely). Her nan says it's «a tiny slip», but it is about 1,800 years out.
fact: An anachronism is something that belongs to another time, such as a Roman soldier with a wristwatch or a Viking with a smartphone.
fact: Potatoes, tomatoes, maize and chocolate reached Europe only after Columbus's voyage of 1492, and horses, which had died out in the Americas long ago, came back only with the Spanish.
fact: Tea reached Europe in the early 1600s, so no Viking, Roman or medieval knight ever drank a cup of tea.
fact: Roman numerals have no zero; zero as a number came from India and reached Europe many centuries after Rome, so a Roman writing «0» is out of time.
fact: The magnetic compass was used by Chinese sailors by about 1100 and by European sailors by about 1200, so Columbus did have one, but the telescope was made only in 1608.
fact: Photography began in the 1820s and 1830s: Napoleon, who died in 1821, was never photographed, but Abraham Lincoln and Taras Shevchenko, who lived on into the 1850s and 1860s, were.
fact: Queen Victoria first rode on a train in 1842 and saw Bell show her the telephone in 1878, but television came only in the 1920s, long after she died in 1901.
fact: Winners at the ancient Olympic Games got a crown of olive leaves; gold, silver and bronze medals at the modern Olympics began in 1904.
fact: Prince Volodymyr of Kyiv, who died in 1015, never saw a printed book: books then were copied by hand, and one of the first printed books in Ukraine, the «Apostol», came out in Lviv in 1574.
fact: Vikings did not wear horned helmets in battle; the horns come from costumes made for an opera in 1876.
fact: Passenger railways began in the 1820s and 1830s, the first cars in 1885–1886, and the first powered aeroplane flew in 1903.
fact: Pocket watches appeared in Europe in the early 1500s and glasses for reading in Italy around 1290, so a knight of 1100 had neither.
fact: Words can be anachronisms too: «OK» was first printed in America in 1839, so a knight or a Roman saying «OK» in a film is out of time.
exercise: What is an anachronism? | Something put into the wrong time | A very old clock kept in a museum | A story that is completely true | A big mistake in a sum with years
exercise: Which is an anachronism in ancient Rome? | A soldier eating a tomato | A soldier eating wheat bread | A soldier marching on a road | A soldier paying with coins
exercise: Which did Columbus really carry in 1492? | A magnetic compass | A brass ship's telescope | A pocket radio | A wristwatch
exercise: When was the telescope made? | In 1608 | In 1492 | In about 1100 | In 44 BC
exercise: Who of these was never photographed? | Napoleon | Abraham Lincoln | Taras Shevchenko | Queen Victoria
exercise: Which could Queen Victoria really have done? | Seen a telephone at work | Watched the television news | Flown in a jet plane | Sent an email to India
exercise: What did ancient Olympic winners get? | A crown of olive leaves | A shiny gold medal | A silver cup with their name | Prize money in paper notes
exercise: When did modern Olympic gold, silver and bronze medals begin? | In 1904 | In 776 BC | In 1066 | In 1492
exercise: Why could Prince Volodymyr not read a printed book? | Printing reached Ukraine over 500 years after him | He did not like reading books of any kind | Books were banned in Kyiv in his time | All the books of his time were in Chinese
exercise: Where do the horns on cartoon Viking helmets come from? | Costumes for an opera in 1876 | Real Viking battle helmets | Roman drawings of Viking kings | Cow horns found on Viking ships
exercise: Why is a Viking drinking tea in a cartoon wrong? | Tea reached Europe only in the 1600s | The Vikings were only allowed to drink milk | Tea was banned in old Norway | Tea was first grown in 1900
exercise: Which scene is NOT an anachronism? | Columbus checking his compass | Cleopatra wearing reading glasses | A Roman writing the number zero | A knight of 1100 with a pocket watch
exercise: Why is a Roman writing «0» out of time? | Roman numerals had no zero | Romans could not count at all | Zero was banned by Caesar | Romans wrote numbers only in Greek
exercise: Which came last? | The first powered aeroplane | The first passenger railways | The first cars | The first photographs
exercise: A film set in 1850 shows a family driving a car. Is that right? | No, cars came in the 1880s | Yes, cars came in the 1820s | Yes, cars are as old as trains | No, cars came only in the 1950s
exercise: What did Rome use for money? | Coins | Paper notes | Bank cards | Cheques
exercise: Why is a knight saying «OK» in a film out of time? | «OK» was first printed in 1839 | «OK» is a Latin word | Knights could not speak at all | «OK» comes from the Vikings
test: ★★★ Lily plays a game: «A Plains warrior on horseback in the year 1300, epic!» What's wrong? | Horses came back to the Americas with the Spanish, after 1492 | Nothing; Plains peoples rode horses for a million years | Only that the horse should really be a camel | Nothing; horses first came from America to Spain
test: ★★★ Noah in Atlanta: «Lincoln in a photo? Fake, y'all, cameras weren't even invented yet.» Fair? | No; photos began decades before Lincoln was president | Yes; the first camera came in 1950 | Yes; every Lincoln photo is a painting | No; Lincoln himself invented the camera
test: ★★★ A cartoon shows Cleopatra snacking on chocolate. «It's giving ancient queen», says Ava. What's the real spot? | Chocolate came from the Americas | Nothing; cocoa grew all over Egypt | Only the shiny wrapper is wrong | Chocolate had been banned in Egypt
test: ★★★ Danny from Liverpool: «Boss film, sound, Columbus with a telescope on deck.» (boss and sound mean great) Spot it? | The telescope came in 1608 | The compass; he had none | The deck; his ships had none | Nothing; it's all spot on
test: ★★★ Ben: «Queen Victoria on a train? Mate, that's an anachronism.» Right? | No, she first rode a train in 1842 | Yes, trains only came in 1950 | Yes, queens never rode on trains | Yes, trains came only after her death
test: ★★★ Your little cousin draws an ancient Olympic winner biting a gold medal. Best fix? | Swap the medal for an olive crown | Swap it for a silver medal | Nothing; the drawing is right | Add a podium and a flag
test: ★★★ Four film scenes. Which one has NO anachronism? | Shevchenko sitting for a photo in 1858 | Napoleon sitting for a photo in 1815 | A Viking drinking tea in 900 | A Roman with a wristwatch
test: ★★★ Grandad Bob: «In 1960 we had a telly, a phone box down the road and no internet.» Any anachronism? | None; all of that fits 1960 | The telly; TV came in 1990 | The phone box; they came in 2000 | No internet; it was everywhere then
test: ★★★ Ruby in Toronto: «Knight in 1100 checks his pocket watch, eh, totally legit.» What's up? | Pocket watches came about 400 years later | Knights could not tell the time in any way at all | Watches were banned in castles back then | Nothing; it's totally legit, eh
test: ★★★ Seren in Swansea: «Vikings in horned helmets, proper history, mun.» Fix it. | The horns come from an opera of 1876 | Only Viking kings ever wore horns | The horns are right, but they were gold | The horns were Roman, not Viking
test: ★★★ A game's Roman market stall sells potatoes, wheat bread, olives and grapes. Which item is the slip? | Potatoes | Wheat bread | Olives | Grapes
test: ★★★ Max: «Prince Volodymyr read a printed Bible in 988, there were literally a million copies.» What's real? | Books were copied by hand then; printing in Ukraine began centuries later | Yes, a million printed copies, he said literally | Printed, yes, but only about ten copies | Printed, yes, in Lviv, in the year 988
exam: Define the term «anachronism». | Something placed in a period to which it does not belong | An exceptionally ancient artefact | A calendar error of a single year | An account wholly free from error
exam: Identify the instrument which Christopher Columbus could genuinely have employed in 1492. | A magnetic compass | An astronomical telescope | A camera | A wristwatch
exam: Select the historical figure of whom no photograph can exist. | Napoleon Bonaparte | President Abraham Lincoln | Taras Shevchenko | Queen Victoria
exam: Determine which foodstuff could not have been eaten in ancient Rome. | The potato | Wheat bread | Olives | Grapes
